      <h2 class="reveal">What to check first</h2>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>Staff information</strong>Names, roles and performance notes need personal-data review.</li>
        <li><strong>Client details</strong>Client names and project specifics may be confidential.</li>
        <li><strong>Consistency</strong>Boards and logs that follow a clear process are easier to use.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section mist">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="reveal">What an operations record teaches a model</h2>
      <p class="reveal">Boards and logs show work as a sequence rather than a result: who held an item, what it waited on, and where it stopped moving. Failures are the useful part, because they are the cases a finished process document leaves out.</p>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>Where a handoff failed</strong>The step that stalled, and who was waiting on whom when it did.</li>
        <li><strong>Which approval held things up</strong>The sign-off that took longest, and what it was checking for.</li>
        <li><strong>How an exception was handled</strong>The case that did not fit the standard path, and the workaround that resolved it.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section teal-soft">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="reveal">What keeps operations records out of scope</h2>
      <p class="reveal">Operational tools hold a mix of process and personnel material. The process is licensable; the personnel material usually is not.</p>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>Performance notes on named staff</strong>Reviews, ratings and disciplinary material are employment data, not process data.</li>
        <li><strong>Client project detail under confidentiality</strong>Boards naming a client and its specifics can be restricted by the same agreement as the work.</li>
        <li><strong>Material under legal hold or audit</strong>Records held for litigation or a regulatory review cannot be moved while that holds.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

